        "no": 58,
        "text": {
          "en": "Article 9\nParagraph (1)\nThe time limit for periodic payment is determined by the Minister of Finance with a time limit that may not exceed fifteen days after the time the tax becomes owed or the end of the Tax Period. Delay in such periodic payment results in the imposition of an administrative sanction in the form of interest of 2% (two per cent) per month.\nParagraph (2)\nAs referred to in Article 3 paragraph (3) letter b, the Taxpayer shall submit the Annual SPT within three months after the end of the Tax Year. If at the time of completing the SPT it turns out that there is still an underpayment of tax owed, that underpayment of tax must be paid in full before the Annual SPT is submitted, for example if the SPT must be submitted on 31 March, the underpayment of tax owed or the final payment must have been settled before the SPT is submitted.\nParagraph (3)\nSufficiently clear.\nParagraph (4)\nThe Director General of Taxes may permit the postponement of the payment of tax owed, even though the due date for payment has been determined. Such relaxation is granted carefully and limited to Taxpayers who are genuinely experiencing liquidity difficulties. It is required to obtain such relaxation that the Taxpayer must submit a written application accompanied by reasons that can be accounted for and convincing."
        },
