        "no": 64,
        "text": {
          "en": "Article 15\nParagraph (1)\nTo accommodate the possibility of the occurrence of a Tax Assessment Letter which turns out to have been determined too low, or of a tax refund that should not have been made, or at the time of a determination in the form of a Tax Overpayment Refund Decision, or of the issuance of a Notification Letter, this Law still gives authority to the Director General of Taxes to issue an Additional Tax Assessment Letter within five years after the time the tax becomes owed, the end of the Tax Period, Part of a Tax Year or Tax Year.\nAn Additional Tax Assessment Letter constitutes a correction of the previous Tax Assessment Letter.\nAn Additional Tax Assessment Letter is only issued if a Tax Assessment Letter has previously been issued. In other words, an Additional Tax Assessment Letter could not possibly be issued without being preceded by the issuance of a Tax Assessment Letter.\nThis paragraph does not only require that there must be new data (novum) in the issuance of an Additional Tax Assessment Letter.\nIn the event that data is still found which was not disclosed at the time of the issuance of the Additional Tax Assessment Letter, or is only newly known, the Additional Tax Assessment Letter may still be issued again by the Director General of Taxes.\nParagraph (2)\nSufficiently clear.\nParagraph (3)\nSufficiently clear.\nParagraph (4)\nIn the event that the Taxpayer is convicted for committing a criminal act in the field of taxation concerning tax whose collection period has expired based on a Court judgment that has obtained permanent legal force, an Additional Tax Assessment Letter is still justified to be issued, although the five-year period as determined in Article 15 paragraph (1) has been exceeded. With the existence of that Court judgment which has obtained permanent legal force, fiscal data is revealed which until then was deliberately not reported by the Taxpayer."
        },
