        "no": 59,
        "text": {
          "en": "Article 14\nParagraph (1) and paragraph (2)\nIn essence, in order to fulfil the tax obligation on income from business and independent work as well as possible, bookkeeping is required; the Law intends to encourage all taxpayers to keep books, but it is also recognised that not all taxpayers are able to keep such books. Taxpayers permitted not to keep complete books include taxpayers whose business turnover or gross receipts amount to less than Rp60,000,000 per year. For them there is a need for an open and fair method, in addition to the need for guidance so that they may later be able and capable of keeping books.\nNorm …\nThe Computation Norm is a guideline that may be used as a method to determine gross turnover or gross receipts and ultimately to determine net income.\nBasically this Computation Norm is used only for the calculation or determination of net income in the case of:\n- the absence of another better calculation basis, namely bookkeeping,\n- the taxpayer's books turning out to be kept incorrectly.\nThe form of the Computation Norm is a percentage or other comparative figure arranged in such a way based on the results of careful research so that it is\n- simple,\n- detailed according to groups of types of business,\n- differentiated into several classifications of city/place,\n- differentiated for taxpayers whose business turnover or gross receipts are less than Rp60,000,000 and those more than Rp60,000,000,\n- a percentage level or comparative figure that is not far from reasonableness, yet can encourage taxpayers to keep books.\nThus the Computation Norm is a tool used in a compelled situation, because there is no other guidance, yet its simplicity, openness and reasonableness can still be accounted for.\nThe Computation Norm greatly helps taxpayers who are not yet able to keep books to calculate the net income that must be stated in the Annual Tax Return. Because taxpayers will determine their own tax, the existence of a benchmark for calculating how much income is declared in advance will be very useful. Only if it is proven that the Annual Tax Return is incorrect, the Director General of Taxes is authorised to determine the tax based on correct data, by applying the Computation Norm plus an administrative sanction in the form of …\nin the form of an increase. The open nature of the Computation Norm, besides facilitating the implementation of obligations for taxpayers, also serves at the same time to prevent arbitrary action by the Tax Administration by estimating income on an unfounded basis.\nThe Computation Norm is prepared and continuously improved and issued by the Director General of Taxes guided by a guideline determined by the Minister of Finance.\nThe guideline determined by the Minister of Finance must contain:\na. the links that must be used to determine the amount of:\n- turnover (number of employees, number of tables for a restaurant business, number of machines for an industrial business, number of rooms for a hotel business and others);\n- gross income (amount of material purchases, amount of employee salaries, and others);\n- net income (amount of actual expenditure or level of living costs and others);\nb. the main points of the method that must be observed in preparing the Computation Norm;\nc. the methods of improving the Computation Norm.\nParagraph (3)\nSufficiently clear.\nParagraph (4)\nA taxpayer as referred to in paragraph (2) that does not notify its choice to calculate net income using the Computation Norm is deemed to keep books.\nIn the event that such taxpayer turns out not to keep books, then the net income is calculated using the Computation Norm and the tax resulting from that calculation is increased by an administrative sanction in the form of an increase, as regulated in paragraph (7).\nWith …\nWith this provision, taxpayers are encouraged to keep good, correct and complete books. Therefore the Computation Norm needs to be prepared as well as possible by taking into account good and efficient enterprises or independent work. For an honest taxpayer whose business does not succeed in obtaining income like a good and efficient enterprise or independent work, the use of the Computation Norm may be detrimental.\nTo avoid the application of a Computation Norm that may be detrimental, the taxpayer may choose to keep good, correct and complete books, so that the tax calculation is based on the actual circumstances in accordance with its books.\nParagraph (5)\nA taxpayer that chooses to calculate its net income using the Computation Norm must naturally be able to show that the amount of turnover from its business or gross receipts from its independent work in a year is less than Rp60,000,000 (sixty million rupiah), which can be proven from the records of gross turnover or gross receipts that it keeps.\nParagraph (6)\nUnder this provision, net income is calculated based on the Computation Norm for taxpayers that:\na. have the obligation to keep books, but do not keep books as stipulated by the law;\nb. have the obligation to keep records of gross turnover or gross receipts, but do not keep records as required;\nc. are not willing to show the books, records and other evidence requested by the Director General of Taxes.\nIt …\nIt should be emphasised that those having the obligation to keep books are taxpayers whose business turnover or gross receipts amount to Rp60,000,000 (sixty million rupiah) per year and taxpayers whose business turnover or gross receipts are less than Rp60,000,000 (sixty million rupiah) per year but who choose or are deemed to choose to keep books.\nParagraph (7)\nThe income tax resulting from net income calculated by applying the Computation Norm as referred to in paragraph (6), plus an administrative sanction in the form of an increase as regulated in Article 13 paragraph (3) of Law Number 6 of 1983 on General Provisions and Tax Procedures."
        },
