        "no": 63,
        "text": {
          "en": "Article 18\nParagraph (1)\nThis Law gives the Minister of Finance the authority to issue a Decision on the amount of the ratio between the debts and capital of an enterprise that may be justified for the purpose of calculating tax.\nIn the business world there is a certain reasonable level of comparison regarding the amount of the ratio between debt and capital (debt equity ratio). If the ratio between debt and capital is very large (above reasonable limits), then the company is actually in an unhealthy condition. In such a case the law determines that there is disguised capital.\nParagraph 2 …\nParagraph 2\nThe purpose of this provision is to prevent tax smuggling, which may occur because of a special relationship. Where there is a special relationship, income may possibly be reported below what it should be, or costs may be charged in excess of what they should be, if transactions occur between the parties concerned. Likewise, disguised capital participation may possibly occur, by declaring such capital participation as debt.\nIn such a case the Director General of Taxes is authorised to redetermine the amount of income and/or costs that should have occurred if there had been no special relationship between those parties.\nLikewise, if based on the examination carried out it turns out that there is participation or disguised capital appearing to be debt, then the Director General of Taxes is authorised to determine such debt as the company's capital. Thus the interest paid in connection with debt that is actually capital participation may not be deducted, while for the shareholder receiving or obtaining it, it is income subject to tax.\nIn Article 6 paragraph (1) letter a, interest is a cost that may be deducted from income, but conversely dividends paid to shareholders may not be deducted.\nParagraph (3)\nLetter a\nA special relationship is deemed to exist if two or more taxpayers are under the same ownership or control. This ownership or control means that the owner of those companies holds a majority of shares that can influence the running of the company. A special relationship may also be formulated as follows:\n- Entity AA …\n- Entity AA has a participation of 25% (twenty-five per cent) in company BB, so AA and BB have a special relationship.\n- A person YY has a 25% (twenty-five per cent) participation in company AA and also a 25% (twenty-five per cent) participation in company BB, so YY, AA and BB have a special relationship.\nLetter b\nBlood relatives in the direct line of descent to the first degree are father, mother and children, while blood relatives in the collateral line to the first degree are siblings. Relatives by marriage in the direct line of descent to the first degree are parents-in-law and stepchildren, while relatives by marriage in the collateral line to the first degree are brothers- or sisters-in-law.\nParagraph (4)\nWhere there are several parties having a special relationship with a participation of 50% (fifty per cent) or more, the lowest rate of 15% (fifteen per cent) may be applied only once.\nWhere one party suffers a loss, the loss may not be compensated against the income of the other party, but vertical compensation as referred to in Article 6 paragraph (3) shall apply."
        },
