        "text": {
          "en": "Article 21\nParagraph (1)\nThose who shall withhold Income Tax, hereinafter called tax withholders, under this provision are:\na. individual companies or corporate entities that are the parent or branch of a company that pay salaries, wages, honorariums and other remuneration to employees or other persons, provided that the work is performed in Indonesia. In the meaning of employer it is not necessary to be a Tax Subject under this Law, but it may also be any person or corporate entity that in an employment relationship pays salaries, wages and so forth;\nb. for example …\nb. for example salaries paid to employees of the Embassy of the Republic of Indonesia abroad, because they are charged to State Finance, must be withheld for Income Tax. The meaning of State Finance includes the Finance of Regional Governments;\nc. a pension fund entity that pays pension payments, whether pension payments paid to retired employees or workers or to their heirs. The meaning of pension includes allowances whether paid periodically or not;\nd. companies or entities, where there are payments to expert personnel or associations of expert personnel as domestic taxpayers for services performed in Indonesia. The meaning of company includes Service Companies, and the meaning of entity includes entities representing foreign states and international bodies.\nParagraph (2)\nWhat is subject to tax withholding is the portion of income each month that exceeds one-twelfth of the non-taxable income as referred to in Article 7. For example, an employee who is married with 3 (three) dependants has non-taxable income of Rp2,880,000 (two million eight hundred and eighty thousand rupiah) or Rp240,000 (two hundred and forty thousand rupiah) each month. If the employee's income is Rp250,000 (two hundred and fifty thousand rupiah) per month, then the income subject to tax withholding is Rp10,000 (ten thousand rupiah).\nParagraph (3)\nIn accordance with the nature of Income Tax as a personal tax and not a tax on property, meaning that the taxpayer's family who are wholly dependent also determine the amount of Income Tax payable, the correctness of the taxpayer's statement letter regarding his family composition is absolutely necessary.\nAs …\nAs a means of comparison, the taxpayer's Family Card issued by the local Regional Government may also be used.\nParagraph (4)\nSufficiently clear.\nParagraph (5)\nSufficiently clear.\nParagraph (6)\nIn the Guidance Book of the Director General of Taxes a table is contained which may be used by employers to withhold the amount of Income Tax that must be remitted to the State Treasury.\nParagraph (7)\nIf the employer has carried out withholding and remittance of Income Tax correctly, then at the end of the tax year the employees or persons whose Income Tax has been withheld are no longer required to submit an Annual Tax Return. In other words, Income Tax that has been withheld correctly is declared final under the provisions of this Law.\nParagraph (8)\nFor employees who have other income besides their salary/wages, they shall fill in and submit an Annual Tax Return. The obligation to submit an Annual Tax Return also applies to those who receive or obtain income from work from more than one employer.\nParagraph (9)\nTo facilitate the implementation of Income Tax withholding by the paying party, the Director General of Taxes issues a Guidance Book on Income Tax Withholding.\nArticle 22 …"
        },
        "noLabel": {
          "en": "Article 66"
        }
